1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,150
Listen to a short talk about Astronomy.

2
00:00:02,150 --> 00:00:06,110
In ancient times, many people believed the earth was a flat disc.

3
00:00:06,110 --> 00:00:11,131
Well over 2000 years ago, the ancient Greek philosophers were able to put forward

4
00:00:11,131 --> 00:00:13,120
two good arguments proving that it was not.

5
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:18,060
Direct observations of heavenly bodies was the basis of both these arguments.

6
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:22,598
First, the Greeks knew that during eclipses of the moon, the earth was between the sun

7
00:00:22,598 --> 00:00:23,140
and the moon,

8
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:28,020
and they saw that during these eclipses, the earth’s shadow on the moon was always round.

9
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:31,090
They realized this could be true only if the earth was spherical.

10
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:35,386
If the earth were a flat disc, then its shadow during eclipses would not

11
00:00:35,386 --> 00:00:39,040
be a perfect circle, it would be stretched out into a long ellipse.

12
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:43,050
The second argument was based on what the Greeks saw during their travels.

13
00:00:43,050 --> 00:00:44,635
They noticed that the North Star,

14
00:00:44,635 --> 00:00:48,368
or Polaris, appeared lower in the sky when they traveled south, in more northerly

15
00:00:48,368 --> 00:00:52,000
regions, the North Star appeared to them to be much higher in the sky.

16
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,377
By the way, it was also from this difference in the apparent position of

17
00:00:55,377 --> 00:01:00,100
the North Star that the Greeks first calculated the approximate distance around the circumference of the earth.

18
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:04,006
a figure recorded in ancient documents says 400000 stadia,

19
00:01:04,006 --> 00:01:06,060
that's the plural of the word stadium.

20
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:10,075
Today, it’s not known exactly what length one stadium represents,

21
00:01:10,075 --> 00:01:14,070
but let’s say it was about 200 meters, the length of many athletic stadiums.

22
00:01:14,070 --> 00:01:20,072
This would make the Greek’s estimate about twice the figure accepted today, a very good estimate for those writing

23
00:01:20,072 --> 00:01:22,240
so long before even the first telescope was invented.
